WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.487
Two pods of the payment service write one payment's status to the same record.

2
00:00:07.487 --> 00:00:11.353
It keeps the write with the highest timestamp: last write wins.

3
00:00:11.567 --> 00:00:14.920
Each pod stamps its writes with its own clock.

4
00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:16.159
pod A's is exact.

5
00:00:16.159 --> 00:00:19.949
pod B's is 15 milliseconds behind, and nobody knows.

6
00:00:20.167 --> 00:00:23.704
At two milliseconds past ten, pod A writes captured.

7
00:00:23.704 --> 00:00:26.153
The record is empty, so it keeps it.

8
00:00:26.367 --> 00:00:30.692
pod B reads captured, then writes refunded 6 milliseconds later.

9
00:00:30.692 --> 00:00:32.652
Its write really came second.

10
00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:36.172
But pod B's clock says 7 milliseconds before ten.

11
00:00:36.172 --> 00:00:40.913
That stamp is lower, so the record drops the refund, and still says OK.

12
00:00:41.133 --> 00:00:42.477
It keeps happening.

13
00:00:42.477 --> 00:00:46.649
pod A's settled is kept, and pod B's chargeback is dropped.

14
00:00:46.649 --> 00:00:50.113
Four OKs, two writes gone, and no error anywhere.

15
00:00:50.333 --> 00:00:54.867
The skew only has to beat the 6 milliseconds between dependent writes.

16
00:00:54.867 --> 00:00:58.688
With 100 milliseconds of skew, half of all writes are lost.

17
00:00:58.933 --> 00:01:02.144
Better NTP shrinks the skew, but never to zero.

18
00:01:02.144 --> 00:01:06.243
Google's Spanner uses a clock that admits its error instead.

19
00:01:06.467 --> 00:01:11.811
It returns an interval, earliest to latest, that contains the true time.

20
00:01:11.811 --> 00:01:14.557
pod A's error bound is 1 millisecond.

21
00:01:14.557 --> 00:01:15.596
pod B's is 16.

22
00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:20.601
To commit, pod A stamps captured with latest: 3 milliseconds past ten.

23
00:01:20.601 --> 00:01:25.333
Then it waits until earliest passes the stamp, and only then says OK.

24
00:01:25.333 --> 00:01:26.636
That's commit wait.

25
00:01:26.867 --> 00:01:30.584
Now the stamp is in the past on every honest clock.

26
00:01:30.584 --> 00:01:35.977
pod B writes refunded at 10 past ten, and its latest is 11, higher than 3.

27
00:01:35.977 --> 00:01:37.361
The refund is kept.

28
00:01:37.567 --> 00:01:41.950
The price is the wait: about twice the bound, on every commit.

29
00:01:41.950 --> 00:01:44.919
pod B waits 32 milliseconds before its OK.

30
00:01:45.133 --> 00:01:48.279
Last write wins trusts two machines' clocks to agree.

31
00:01:48.279 --> 00:01:52.315
To order writes safely, don't compare them, or wait out their error.
